    <briefdescription>
<para>Deprecated overloaded operators for test comparisons. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The <computeroutput>mt::operators</computeroutput> namespace defines the comparison operators <computeroutput>==</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>!=</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>&lt;</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>&gt;</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>&lt;=</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>&gt;=</computeroutput>, and the logical operators <computeroutput>and</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>or</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>not</computeroutput>, for the framework types. After <computeroutput>using namespace mt::operators</computeroutput>, a check such as <computeroutput>t.expect (mt::to_i{ compute_answer () } == 42_i)</computeroutput> reports the values of its operands, as the function comparators do.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Deprecated</title><para>The operators are deprecated since 6.0.0, in favour of the function comparators <computeroutput>mt::eq()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::ne()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::lt()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::le()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::gt()</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>mt::ge()</computeroutput>, and of the logical functions <computeroutput>mt::all_of()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::any_of()</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>mt::negate()</computeroutput>, which check and report the plain values, without literal suffixes or wrappers.</para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="par"><title>Limitations</title><para><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The operators apply only when at least one operand is a framework type (see <computeroutput>extension::is_op</computeroutput>): a deprecated literal, such as <computeroutput>1_i</computeroutput>, or a deprecated wrapper, such as <computeroutput>mt::to_i{ value }</computeroutput>. When neither operand is, the standard operator is selected; it returns a plain <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput>, so a failed check cannot show the operands.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>The overloaded <computeroutput>and</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>or</computeroutput> operators evaluate both operands before the check, so they do not short-circuit like the built-in ones.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</simplesect>
